[{:db/id 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" "question-6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9b7d5ac-eef6-5e03-9395-4cad5505dd2d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e9b7d5ac-eef6-5e03-9395-4cad5505dd2d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b7d5ac-eef6-5e03-9395-4cad5505dd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3575b062-a274-5475-804d-55979057dd00" "answer-eef188a5-b79b-535e-a698-3abfd5aa7c90" "answer-78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" "answer-ef9201b9-0d51-54ab-bcd2-eefc57d9346d" "answer-8e23f760-5ac7-5ae1-99ac-01f57b186e7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3575b062-a274-5475-804d-55979057dd00" :answer/id #uuid "3575b062-a274-5475-804d-55979057dd00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eef188a5-b79b-535e-a698-3abfd5aa7c90" :answer/id #uuid "eef188a5-b79b-535e-a698-3abfd5aa7c90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" :answer/id #uuid "78caf0fc-34c9-58ec-aada-68ca4ec8235e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef9201b9-0d51-54ab-bcd2-eefc57d9346d" :answer/id #uuid "ef9201b9-0d51-54ab-bcd2-eefc57d9346d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "l only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e23f760-5ac7-5ae1-99ac-01f57b186e7b" :answer/id #uuid "8e23f760-5ac7-5ae1-99ac-01f57b186e7b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "ll only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/id #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104574" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$\n- $G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$\n- $H=(8x-3y)\\,i+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd4eb193-01d2-584e-8123-beed2a93cb45"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd4eb193-01d2-584e-8123-beed2a93cb45" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4eb193-01d2-584e-8123-beed2a93cb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6dd4d74c-d28c-5067-b253-93fff9b615be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96aeda3b-433c-5c5a-a89e-38dda14fc2d1" "answer-6dd4d74c-d28c-5067-b253-93fff9b615be" "answer-dc0937dc-40e4-5230-9016-9a4210085b2c" "answer-ccb3baff-2a23-5160-bf8a-97ba1ff3048b" "answer-b3d7b4b6-5f16-50df-84c9-3952694f88d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96aeda3b-433c-5c5a-a89e-38dda14fc2d1" :answer/id #uuid "96aeda3b-433c-5c5a-a89e-38dda14fc2d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dd4d74c-d28c-5067-b253-93fff9b615be" :answer/id #uuid "6dd4d74c-d28c-5067-b253-93fff9b615be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc0937dc-40e4-5230-9016-9a4210085b2c" :answer/id #uuid "dc0937dc-40e4-5230-9016-9a4210085b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccb3baff-2a23-5160-bf8a-97ba1ff3048b" :answer/id #uuid "ccb3baff-2a23-5160-bf8a-97ba1ff3048b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3d7b4b6-5f16-50df-84c9-3952694f88d8" :answer/id #uuid "b3d7b4b6-5f16-50df-84c9-3952694f88d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5445d5ff-8221-5ab1-9733-fcfbdf6c3d6a" :activity/id #uuid "5445d5ff-8221-5ab1-9733-fcfbdf6c3d6a" :activity/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050983 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-e11a097e-2d0f-542d-a29a-07a9eed3fead" "step-b4f0c749-c70e-5cd0-8997-a35907ffaa6a" "step-a79f93a0-28bb-5df3-afcd-772f79c2b956"] :activity/first-step "step-e11a097e-2d0f-542d-a29a-07a9eed3fead" :db/ensure [